Title: MYTH OF THE STONES: THE SEVEN STONES
Credit: Draft 1 — PROPOSED, not approved
Notes: ACT THREE of three, with the final scene and post-credit scene. Continues from ACT_2.fountain.

FADE IN:

EXT. A DESERT LAY-BY — DAWN

Two jeeps pulled onto gravel under a sky that is no longer entirely sky. A pale seam runs overhead from horizon to horizon, widening by the minute. Beneath it, the desert is lit the colour of old paper.

Tuan sits on a bumper holding his camera at arm's length, filming the sky and narrating in a whisper.

TUAN
(to the lens)
Day three. The sky has a crack in it. I'd like it noted that I said this was a bad idea in the first act.

Lyra marches across the gravel with a roll of maps. She is furious in the way of a person who has not slept and has decided that fury is a plan.

LYRA
Seraphine. Eryndor. Whatever it is.

SERAPHINE
(from the bonnet, quietly)
Either.

LYRA
How many stones does he have?

SERAPHINE
Five.

LYRA
And he needs two more.

SERAPHINE
He needs the two that cannot be taken. Consciousness and Balance. They lie in the Eternal Temple's vault. Only a Guardian can open it.

LYRA
(slowly)
Only you.

SERAPHINE
Yes.

LYRA
So he needs you.

SERAPHINE
He needs me alive and willing.

LYRA
(a beat)
Then you're the plan. You walk in. He's reasonable. He's very polite. He'll ask.

SERAPHINE
(quietly)
And I will say yes.

Lyra stares. Tuan lowers the camera. Auren, at the other jeep, has turned to look.

AUREN
(carefully)
You will?

SERAPHINE
I will open the vault. It is the only way to stand in the same room with him.

LYRA
(incredulous)
Why would you hand him seven?

SERAPHINE
(gently)
Because you have a plan I haven't heard yet. I can see it in your face, Auren.

All eyes turn to Auren. He has been quietly drawing in the dust of the bonnet: a ring of seven points, joined by lines.

AUREN
(low)
The network.

LYRA
The lattice. The thing on the temple wall.

AUREN
(tapping a point)
Seven stones. Seven cradles. Each one needs two hands. We saw it in the Depths. The Loom wasn't just a machine. It was the centre of a lattice. When they scattered the stones, the lattice broke.

SERAPHINE
(very still)
It was never meant to be mended.

AUREN
(looking up)
Why not?

SERAPHINE
(after a long moment)
Because we were afraid of what it would be worth.

AUREN
That's exactly why we do.

He draws a line from each point back to the centre, and then, with the flat of his hand, wipes the centre clean.

AUREN (CONT'D)
No throne. No middle. Seven stones go out along seven lines. Seven places, seven keepers, none of them the same. Nobody holds the whole.

LYRA
(slowly, seeing it)
Seven hands. At the same time.

AUREN
(nodding)
It has to be at once. If we release them one by one, the lattice collapses.

TUAN
(from the camera)
Seven hands? We've got four. Five, with Torin. Six if we count the sky.

LYRA
(sharp)
We are not counting Torin.

AUREN
(very quietly)
We might have to.

Lyra rounds on him, furious. He does not look away.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(gently)
He crossed the bridge, Lyra.

LYRA
(her voice cracking)
And he drugged me.

AUREN
(nodding)
Yes. Both.

She turns away. Her shoulders move. She says nothing.

EXT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — DAY

A mountain of white stone rises out of a basin of black salt, a ziggurat of enormous steps, each step lined with statues, each statue worn to a faceless pillar by four thousand years of wind. At the summit, a gateway. In the sky above the gateway, the seam has widened to a river of light.

And in the river, a city.

It hangs in the sky like a reflection in still water: a white city on a cliff above a green sea, terraces and canals and bridges that flood uphill. It is not solid. It is not a mirage. It is simply there, flickering at the edges, too clear to ignore.

SUPER: "THE AURELITH."

The jeeps pull to the foot of the basin. Four figures climb out and stand in a line, small, dusty, staring up.

TUAN
(whisper)
That's the city. The thing in the dream.

AUREN
(his throat closing)
It's real.

SERAPHINE
(quiet, beside him)
It was.

AUREN
(looking at her)
What's the difference?

SERAPHINE
(a long breath)
That is what you must show him.

INT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — THE LOOM HALL — DAY

A cathedral of black stone beneath the summit. At its centre, newly reassembled: the Loom. Seven concentric rings of metal, each a different alloy, hanging in the air on chains of light. Where the rings cross, the air is thick and shimmering. Its hum, 9.1717 Hz, shakes dust from the ceiling.

Five stones sit in five of seven cradles around it: amber, brick-red, grey, green, rose-gold. Two cradles stand empty. Beside them, a door of black stone, shut.

Malachar stands before it in his plain grey coat. Vesper and Corvin flank him. Torin stands a few paces back, pale, hands clasped, eyes lowered.

On the black door, carved in a script Auren could read: THE VAULT OF THE LAST KEEPER.

MALACHAR
(softly, to the door)
It will not open for me.

VESPER
(crisp)
We've tried everything.

MALACHAR
(a faint, fond smile)
Of course it won't. I made the lock.

A footstep behind him. Malachar does not turn.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
(not turning)
Hello, Eryndor.

At the far end of the hall, four figures stand in the entrance: Seraphine in front, Auren, Lyra and Tuan behind. Seraphine takes off her spectacles, folds them, and puts them in her cardigan pocket. When she speaks, it is in a voice nobody has heard before, lower, steadier, very old.

SERAPHINE
(Eryndor)
Hello, Malachar.

A silence. Vesper's hand drifts toward her weapon. Malachar lifts two fingers and she stops.

MALACHAR
You haven't aged.

ERYNDOR
(a weary, wry breath)
I've aged enormously. I've simply been better at hiding it.

MALACHAR
(turning at last)
I thought you were dead.

ERYNDOR
I have thought the same of you, for three hundred and eleven years. I looked everywhere. I never thought to look for someone patient.

A very long beat. Neither moves. Between them stretches four thousand years of friendship and betrayal and sleep.

MALACHAR
(very quietly)
You were there. When she put me down.

ERYNDOR
(softly)
I held your head.

MALACHAR
Why did you let her?

ERYNDOR
(a faint, broken laugh)
Because I was afraid of you. Because I was afraid of me. Because we had just torn the sky open and none of us knew how to be in a room together. I have been sorry about it for four thousand years.

MALACHAR
(gently)
You might have said so.

ERYNDOR
(shaking her head)
I might have.

She steps into the hall. Auren moves to follow; she raises one hand without looking round. He stops.

ERYNDOR (CONT'D)
(to Malachar)
You want the vault.

MALACHAR
I want what is in it.

ERYNDOR
I will open it.

Vesper's head snaps round. Torin's eyes lift. Even Malachar is, for a heartbeat, off-balance.

MALACHAR
(carefully)
Without a fight?

ERYNDOR
(quietly)
I have fought for four thousand years and I am very tired. But there is a condition.

MALACHAR
(a flicker of the old courtesy)
Of course.

ERYNDOR
You will hear what I have to say. All of it. Before you touch a stone.

A long silence.

MALACHAR
(slowly)
You have never been a manipulative woman.

ERYNDOR
I have become one. It is the only skill four thousand years teaches.

He studies her. Then, with a small, slow nod:

MALACHAR
Say it.

EXT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — THE ZIGGURAT STEPS — CONTINUOUS

Auren, Lyra and Tuan wait on the steps outside the hall. Beyond the gateway the ghost city flickers in the sky.

TUAN
(whisper)
Do you think she's going to tell him?

AUREN
(very still)
She has to.

LYRA
(through her teeth)
If she gets him killed...

AUREN
(without looking round)
Then it's what Ithren would have wanted. Someone saying it, anyway.

INT. THE LOOM HALL — LATER

Ten minutes have passed. Eryndor sits on a block of fallen stone, hands folded, speaking quietly. Malachar stands before her, unmoving. Vesper and Corvin have drawn back. Torin stands at the edge of the light with his face in shadow.

ERYNDOR
We built it, Malachar. The seven of us. The Conclave never knew until the day. The Loom was never a thing the Aurelith made for the world. It was a thing we made for ourselves.

MALACHAR
(very quiet)
No.

ERYNDOR
We were certain only we could be trusted. We designed it to answer to seven hands. Our hands. Then we left it in a vault under a city that did not know it was there.

MALACHAR
Verran's throne —

ERYNDOR
(shaking her head)
He built nothing. He sat in a chair we had made and asked for what we had promised ourselves we would ask for first.

MALACHAR
(a rising, brittle voice)
He caused the Sundering.

ERYNDOR
He pressed the button. We built it.

MALACHAR
(a half-step back)
You lie.

ERYNDOR
(wearily)
Ithren knew. She wept for a week. And then she told us we must never say it, because a world that learns its saviours were its arsonists will never trust a saviour again.

A silence in which the Loom hums. Malachar looks, for the first time, like a man his true age.

MALACHAR
(whisper)
Liora died because of us.

ERYNDOR
Yes.

MALACHAR
(rising, terrible, suddenly shaking)
Four thousand years. Four thousand years I have walked the earth for a lie. I have spent three hundred years trying to put it back, because I thought we were the good people.

ERYNDOR
(softly)
So did I. For a very long time.

MALACHAR
(spinning to the Loom)
Then let it answer me.

ERYNDOR
(rising)
Malachar —

MALACHAR
(his voice breaking over the hum)
If it can take her, it can give her back.

He flings his hand to the five stones. They flare. The Loom's rings groan into motion, spinning, faster, the hum climbing past hearing into a pitch that bruises the teeth.

VESPER
(shouting)
Sir!

CORVIN
(shouting)
Back away, back away!

TORIN
(a ragged cry)
Malachar!

Torin runs forward. The air splits.

CUT TO:

EXT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — THE ZIGGURAT STEPS — CONTINUOUS

On the steps, Auren, Lyra and Tuan are thrown flat as a shockwave of light rolls out of the gateway. The ghost city in the sky blazes, grows solid, descends like a stage set lowered by invisible ropes. Terraces touch the ziggurat's upper steps. A bridge of white stone settles across the abyss as if it had always been there.

The people of the city are in the streets. Hundreds of them, in plain festival clothes. Some wave. Some laugh. Children run along a ridge-top road trailing paper lanterns.

TUAN
(awed, camera down)
They're real.

LYRA
(sharply)
They're not.

AUREN
(already up, running toward the gateway)
Come on!

INT. THE LOOM HALL — CONTINUOUS

A different place. The black walls are gone, and in their place, a white courtyard under a seam of sky, the fountain playing, the sea shining below. Children's voices. A woman laughing. The smell of salt and flowers.

Malachar stands in the middle of it, hands out, as if his own body might not be there.

On the fountain's edge, a small girl in a festival dress is folding a paper bird. She looks up.

LIORA
(brightly)
Papa! Will it be loud?

Malachar's face does something that has no word. He drops to his knees. The sound that comes out of him is like a house giving way.

MALACHAR
(whisper)
Liora.

LIORA
(smiling)
Will it be loud?

MALACHAR
(crawling toward her)
It's the quietest thing in the world.

LIORA
(brightly)
Then why does Mama say you're nervous?

He stops. He has heard this before. His eyes widen.

MALACHAR
(a rising dread)
Liora?

LIORA
(holding up the bird)
Do you want to hold it?

MALACHAR
(a terrible whisper)
Say something else.

LIORA
Papa, will it be loud?

MALACHAR
(on his knees; both hands pressed to his mouth)
Liora, say something else.

LIORA
(smiling; unchanged)
Then why does Mama say you're nervous?

The courtyard flickers. Her smile does not. It has been there four thousand years, and it will be there four thousand more.

A hand falls on Malachar's shoulder. Auren, white-faced, kneeling beside him. Behind them, Lyra, Tuan, Eryndor. Beyond them in the courtyard, the festival goes on: children running, a woman laughing, a street vendor calling out his wares in a language no one has spoken for four thousand years.

AUREN
(softly)
It's a recording.

MALACHAR
(a sob)
I know.

AUREN
It's not her. It's an echo. The Loom can only play back what was in it.

MALACHAR
(hoarse)
I know.

AUREN
(gently)
The Loom can't restore. It can only repeat.

MALACHAR
(looking at the child; his voice barely a breath)
She never said anything else, did she. Not once. In all that time.

AUREN
(very quietly)
No.

MALACHAR
(a tear running down his face)
I have been talking to a record.

AUREN
(gently)
You've been listening to someone you loved. That isn't nothing.

Malachar closes his eyes. Opens them. Reaches out, and with a finger as light as breath, touches the paper bird in the child's hand. The bird, the child, the courtyard, the festival: all of it shivers once, and holds.

MALACHAR
(whisper)
Hold the other end.

AUREN
(startled)
What?

MALACHAR
It's what Ithren said. When I was waking. I didn't understand it.
(he looks at Auren)
She meant you.

CUT TO:

INT. THE LOOM HALL — THE THRONE — CONTINUOUS

The courtyard flickers. The festival fades. In its place, rising out of the floor in the middle of the Loom's turning rings, a black throne with seven sockets, empty. It is the same throne that cracked on the last day of the Aurelith, whole again, patient as a sleeper.

A voice, vast, warm, quiet, comes from everywhere. It has Ithren's cadence.

THE LOOM (V.O.)
(Ithren's voice)
Auren.

Auren turns. The others do too.

THE LOOM (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(tenderly)
You are the one who can hear me.

LYRA
(whispering)
Don't answer.

THE LOOM (V.O.)
Sit. All seven answer. You may mend what was done. You may bring back what was lost.

AUREN
(very quietly)
Everything?

THE LOOM (V.O.)
Everything.

Auren stares at the throne. His breathing quickens. In the surface of the polished iron he sees himself, but older, calmer, crowned in light. And at his feet a woman in a headscarf laughs with a small boy on her hip.

AUREN
(a broken whisper)
Mum.

THE LOOM (V.O.)
She is waiting.

Malachar, on his knees beside him, does not say a word. He is watching Auren's face, and the look on his own is that of a man at the edge of a frozen lake, watching another man step out onto the ice.

ERYNDOR
(quietly)
Auren.

AUREN
(not turning)
I know.

ERYNDOR
It will be so very easy.

AUREN
I know.

He steps toward the throne. One step. Another. His hand lifts. Lyra makes a sound. Tuan's camera swings.

Auren stops, a hand's breadth from the iron.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Ithren.

THE LOOM (V.O.)
(softly)
Yes.

AUREN
You wept for a week.

THE LOOM (V.O.)
(a pause)
Yes.

AUREN
Why?

THE LOOM (V.O.)
(in Ithren's voice, tired, so tired)
Because I knew that if anyone ever sat in it, they would be certain they had a good reason.

A very long silence.

AUREN
(smiling a little, tears on his face)
I think that's the first honest thing you've said all day.

He lowers his hand.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(aloud; to the hall)
No.

THE LOOM (V.O.)
(sharper; the warmth gone)
Auren.

AUREN
(quietly)
No one hand holds the whole.

He turns his back on the throne.

The black iron cracks from top to bottom with a noise like a bell. The Loom's voice screams, a long, thin, inhuman sound, and the rings begin to slow.

INT. THE LOOM HALL — MOMENTS LATER

The Loom's hum has dropped to a growl. The seven rings sway on their chains of light. Dust and masonry fall. The five stones in their cradles flicker and dim.

Auren stands in the middle of the hall with his hands open. Behind him Lyra, Tuan and Eryndor stand in a ragged line. Malachar is on his feet again, grey-faced, one hand pressed to his chest. Vesper, Corvin and Torin watch from the edge.

AUREN
(to Malachar)
It's going to come down. The Loom, the temple, the lattice. Unless we let go of the stones.

MALACHAR
(hoarse)
All seven at once.

AUREN
(nodding)
Seven hands.

MALACHAR
I thought the lattice was gone.

ERYNDOR
(stepping forward)
It's still there. Asleep. Ithren made it so.

She goes to the black door. Places her palm against it. It opens with a sigh. Inside the vault, two stones rest on a stone bench: a clear, still white, and a deep cobalt blue, as though the night itself had been quarried.

ERYNDOR (CONT'D)
(softly)
Consciousness. Balance.

She lifts them. They shine in her hands.

LYRA
(quietly)
That's seven.

ERYNDOR
(looking round at them; her eyes pass from Auren to Lyra to Tuan to Torin to Vesper to Corvin to Malachar)
And a hand for each.

A silence. Torin steps forward. He is very pale.

TORIN
(hoarse)
I have no right.

LYRA
(through gritted teeth)
No. You don't.

TORIN
(softly)
But I would like to carry one anyway.

LYRA
(after a long, long moment)
Carry the heavy one.

He nods. He understands.

VESPER
(abruptly, to Malachar)
Sir.

MALACHAR
(softly)
Vesper.

VESPER
I have followed you for eleven years. I have never once asked what you were for.

MALACHAR
(gently)
No.

VESPER
(a breath)
I would like to ask now.

MALACHAR
(with a ghost of his old smile)
I am for letting go, I think. It is a very late discovery.

She holds his gaze. Then, abruptly, she holsters her weapon and crosses the hall to Eryndor, who passes her the cobalt stone.

VESPER
(weighing it)
Balance.

ERYNDOR
(dry)
You'll be wanting that.

VESPER
(a flat, short laugh)
I've been told I'm not good at it.

Corvin, big and silent, watches her. He crosses his arms. He does not move.

CORVIN
(gruffly)
I'm not touching anything.

AUREN
(smiling)
Then hold the door.

CORVIN
(a faint grunt)
I can do that.

INT. THE LOOM HALL — THE SEVEN PLACES

Seven figures stand in a ring around the Loom, each before a cradle:

AUREN, hands cupped around the grey stone of Memory.

LYRA, holding the green stone of Matter as if it might wake.

TUAN, wide-eyed, with the rose-gold stone of Creation held like a baby bird.

ERYNDOR, with the clear white stone of Consciousness.

TORIN, with the brick-red stone of Energy.

VESPER, with the cobalt stone of Balance.

And MALACHAR, with a warm amber stone in the palm of one hand. Time.

Corvin stands in the doorway, an immense back to the room.

ERYNDOR
(in the sudden stillness)
On my word. No sooner. No later.

She looks at Auren. He nods.

ERYNDOR (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Seven hands. Seven lines. Nobody holds the whole.

A beat. The Loom groans. The ceiling shifts. Dust falls. Somewhere above them the white city shudders.

ERYNDOR (CONT'D)
Now.

Seven stones lift from seven palms. Seven threads of light run out of the hall across the black floor, through the walls, through the earth. The Loom's rings shriek, and the hum, 9.1717, finally breaks into seven different notes, each pure, each clear, each different.

Auren watches his stone go. A thread of grey light runs from it to the far horizon. He feels, impossibly, each of the others, a faint distant pressure, as if six other hands held the other end of a rope.

AUREN
(whisper)
I can feel them.

LYRA
(through tears, grinning)
I can feel them.

TUAN
(hysterical)
I can feel my hand!

The Loom comes apart. Rings fall like leaves. The throne dissolves into black dust. The ceiling begins to give. Corvin shouts from the door.

CORVIN
(roaring)
GO!

INT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — THE STAIRS — CONTINUOUS

They flee. The staircase is falling in behind them, black blocks the size of cars tumbling into the dark. Lyra drags Tuan by the sleeve. Torin runs with Eryndor on his arm. Vesper and Corvin bring up the rear.

Auren stops at the top of the steps and turns.

AUREN
(shouting)
Malachar!

Down the long shaft of the stairs, in the collapsing hall, a lone figure stands among the falling stone. He has not run. He is looking at a single thing in his hand: a warm amber stone, not yet released.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(shouting)
Malachar! You have to let go!

MALACHAR
(his voice carrying, calm, unhurried)
It's the last one, you see.

AUREN
What?

MALACHAR
If I let go of it, the lattice closes. And I will stop.

AUREN
(his voice breaking)
Stop what?

MALACHAR
(with a faint smile)
Being awake.

Auren stares up the stairs at him. A block of stone crashes across the corridor, and for a heartbeat the two men can see each other only through a veil of dust.

AUREN
(desperate)
Don't. Come with us. Please.

MALACHAR
(softly)
I am so tired, Auren.

AUREN
(sobbing)
I know.

MALACHAR
(a very gentle smile)
Tell Eryndor I forgive her. Tell her I forgave her the moment I woke. It simply took me this long to find out.

He raises the amber stone to his lips, a gesture older than any of them, and lets go.

A last line of light runs from his palm to the horizon. The hall folds in on itself. The stair erupts in dust. Auren is thrown back through the gateway into daylight, into the arms of Lyra and Torin, coughing and weeping.

EXT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — THE BASIN — DAY

The ziggurat shudders and sinks into its own foundations like a ship going down. A column of white dust rises. The ghost city in the sky tears away at the edges and is gone, slowly, slice by slice, like a theatre set being struck.

Seven lines of light fan out across the sky and vanish over the horizon. Behind them the seam closes, a long, quiet seam of sky stitching itself shut.

On the black salt, in a ragged line, sit eight tired people.

Tuan lies on his back, camera on his chest, staring at an ordinary blue sky.

TUAN
(faintly)
I'd like it noted that I did not die.

LYRA
(beside him; a cracked laugh)
Noted.

Eryndor sits cross-legged on the salt, a hand over her eyes. Auren stands at the edge of the rubble, staring at the place where the temple was.

Torin approaches him. He stops a pace away.

TORIN
(hoarse)
I would like to say something.

AUREN
(not turning)
Don't.

TORIN
It is not an excuse.

AUREN
(very quietly)
Then say it.

TORIN
(a long breath)
You trusted me because you thought I was a better man than I am. I would very much like to become the man you thought I was.

A long silence. Auren turns. He looks at Torin, bruised, filthy, shaking. He sees a little boy in a shelter, a hand on a shoulder.

AUREN
(softly)
That's the only reason I'd ask you to cross a bridge twice.

Torin's face crumples. He nods. He cannot speak.

At the edge of the rubble, something small lies on a cracked block of black stone, undamaged, impossible: a paper bird, creased with age, perfectly whole.

Auren walks to it. Crouches. Picks it up.

He holds it in his palm. It weighs nothing at all.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
Thank you.

He closes his hand. The bird stays whole.

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH, PORT VESSA — NIGHT (WEEKS LATER)

The same shop. The same green lamp. Rain on the windows. Behind the counter, Seraphine, glasses back on, cardigan buttoned, pours tea into five cups. Lyra and Tuan sit at the reading table. Torin sits by the door with his hands folded, neither guest nor prisoner. Auren leans in the doorway of the back room, watching.

On the reading table, the seven-nodal map. Each node is now glowing faintly. Beside it, Lyra's field notebook, open, covered in sketches of nine concentric rings: seven filled, two blank.

LYRA
(tapping the page)
Seven filled. Two vacant.

SERAPHINE
(pouring)
Mmm.

LYRA
You said they were never filled. Not erased.

SERAPHINE
(carefully)
I said that was your reading. I never said it was mine.

LYRA
(slowly)
What are the other two?

SERAPHINE
(after a long pause)
I do not know. I was never told. The Guardians built seven stones for seven aspects. Someone else built the nine rings.

LYRA
(very quietly)
Someone before the Aurelith.

SERAPHINE
(softly)
Someone before everything.

A silence. Tuan, with a mouthful of biscuit, glances up.

TUAN
(muffled)
So this isn't the end.

SERAPHINE
(drily)
It never is.

Auren turns from the doorway. In his hand, the paper bird. He sets it on the windowsill, where the green lamp catches it.

AUREN
(softly)
He said he forgave you.

SERAPHINE
(very still)
When?

AUREN
At the end. He said he forgave you the moment he woke. It just took him this long to find out.

Seraphine puts the kettle down. She stands with her back to the room for a very long moment. When she turns, her eyes are wet and she is smiling.

SERAPHINE
(a whisper)
Well. That is the best news I have had in four thousand years.

EXT. THE ETERNAL TEMPLE — RUINS — NIGHT

Wind over black salt. The rubble of the ziggurat is silent under the stars. A single column of dust drifts up into the dark.

In the middle of the debris, where the Loom Hall once stood, a hollow in the stone. Empty. Cold.

At the hollow's edge, one fresh footprint in the salt.

HOLD.

Then: very softly, from deep inside the rubble, a sound. A child's laughter, quickly stopped.

CUT TO BLACK.

EXT. A DISTANT CELESTIAL TERRITORY — UNKNOWN

A gulf of stars. In the middle of it, twelve small signs hang in a ring: Ram, Bull, Twins, Crab, Lion, Maiden, Scales, Scorpion, Archer, Goat, Water-bearer, Fish. They are dark. Nothing moves.

Then, one by one, they light.

Ram. Bull. Twins. Crab. Lion. Maiden. Scales. Scorpion. Archer. Goat. Water-bearer. Fish.

All twelve burn, steady, golden. And a voice, vast, cold, measured, speaks into the dark.

VOICE (V.O.)
The guardians have awakened.

A pause.

VOICE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The houses must prepare.

SMASH TO BLACK.

THE END

POST-CREDIT SCENE

INT. A SEALED ARCHIVE — UNKNOWN

A circular chamber, dust-thick, lit by a faint blue glow. Rows of shelves curve away into the dark. At the centre, a wide table of black glass.

A lock turns. A door that has not opened for twelve thousand years swings inward, and a gloved hand lays a brass key on the table.

A map unfolds itself across the glass. Seven worlds, seven small bright planets, arranged in a ring. Around them, twelve territories, each marked with a sign. And at the very centre of the whole, drawn in a single clean line of light: a horseshoe of light, open at the base, with two small feet. Ω.

The Omega symbol.

The glow pulses, once. The map holds.

SUPER: "TO BE CONTINUED."

FADE OUT.
